er the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y=-11.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Its center is $(1,-3)$\nII. The length of the major axis  is $2\\sqrt{2}$\nIII. The length of the minor axis is $4$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395869 1072 #uuid "fd2cdae2-5e26-5778-ab7e-65d79d794e32" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395869 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y & =-11 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-2x]+2[{y}^{2}+6y] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+2[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-1]+2[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =-11+1+18 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{2}>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  I and III are true, and II is false." 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395871 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395872 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395873 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395874 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395870 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395875 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395870 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395870 1077 "selection" 13194139533466 1